<exercisegroup cols="3" xml:id="exercisegroup-orthog-dot-prod-sign-from-angle-R2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Sign of the dot product</title>

<introduction>

	<sidebyside widths="35% 31.4%" margins="0% 11%"> <!-- 11% is approximately one-third of the blank space remaining -->

		<p>
		At right is a diagram of several vectors in <m> \R^2 </m>.
		Based on the diagram,
		in each case state whether the dot product result should be positive, negative, or zero.
		</p>

		<image width="31.4%">
			<latex-image label="image-exercisegroup-orthog-dot-prod-sign-from-angle-R2">
				<xi:include href="dot-prod-sign-from-angle-R2.d/diagram.tex" parse="text" />
			</latex-image>
			<shortdescription> Diagram of several two-dimensional vectors. </shortdescription>
			<description><p>
				A diagram consisting of six vectors at a right angle in the <m>xy</m>-plane.
				A directed line segment labelled as vector <m> \uvec{v} </m> extends from the origin to the point <m> (9,2) </m>.
				A second directed line segment labelled as vector <m> \uvec{w} </m> extends from the origin to the point <m> (-4,9/2) </m>.
				Where these vectors meet at the origin forms a right angle.
			</p></description>
		</image>

	</sidebyside>

</introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m> \udotprod{a}{b} </m></p></statement>

	<answer><p> The angle is acute, so the dot product result should be positive. </p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m> \udotprod{f}{d} </m></p></statement>

	<answer><p> The angle is right, so the dot product result should be zero. </p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m> \udotprod{h}{e} </m></p></statement>

	<answer><p> The angle is obtuse, so the dot product result should be negative. </p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m> \udotprod{a}{f} </m></p></statement>

	<answer><p>
		The angle is straight but the vectors are oppositely directed,
		so the dot product result should be negative.
	</p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m> \udotprod{f}{g} </m></p></statement>

	<answer><p> The angle is acute, so the dot product result should be positive. </p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m> \udotprod{c}{c} </m></p></statement>

	<answer><p> The angle is zero, so the dot product result should be positive. </p></answer>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
